From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc2: gadget: NAK OUT endpoints until a transfer is started

dwc2_hsotg_ep_enable() activates a non-isochronous OUT endpoint without
setting NAK, and after a core soft reset its NAK status is clear. If the
function driver queues no request for a while (a USB serial port that
nobody on the device has opened, while the host writes to it), the core
accepts the host's packets for that endpoint into the RxFIFO that all OUT
endpoints share. In DMA mode nothing reads them out until a request for
that endpoint is queued, so every OUT endpoint of the gadget stops. Seen
on an SG2002 (DWC_otg 4.20a, descriptor DMA): GINTSTS.RXFLVL stuck at 1,
GRXSTSR naming that endpoint with a 512 byte OUT data packet, the USB
network receiving no frame while the host held one write to the serial
port; the same with buffer DMA (4 of 4 tries).

Set NAK when the endpoint is enabled; starting a transfer clears it
(DXEPCTL_CNAK in dwc2_hsotg_start_req()). With this the host's writes are
NAKed and the other endpoints keep working (descriptor DMA: 50 of 50
pings over the USB network during a 40 s write; 3 of 3 short tries;
buffer DMA: 4 of 4).

diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c b/drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c
@@ -4196,6 +4196,16 @@
 	if (index && !hs_ep->isochronous)
 		epctrl |= DXEPCTL_SETD0PID;
 
+	/*
+	 * NAK on an OUT endpoint until a transfer is started on it. Otherwise
+	 * the core accepts the host's packets for it into the RxFIFO, shared
+	 * by all OUT endpoints, before there is a buffer for them; with DMA
+	 * nothing takes them out, and every OUT endpoint stops until this one
+	 * gets a request.
+	 */
+	if (!dir_in && !hs_ep->isochronous)
+		epctrl |= DXEPCTL_SNAK;
+
 	/* WA for Full speed ISOC IN in DDMA mode.
 	 * By Clear NAK status of EP, core will send ZLP
 	 * to IN token and assert NAK interrupt relying
